TY  - RPRT
TI  - Reading The Room: How Implied User Trust Shapes Self-Limitation In Conversational LLMs
AB  - Frontier conversational language models routinely limit their own autonomy, asking for clarification, hedging, deferring, and seeking confirmation rather than acting independently. Recent work characterises this restraint as a property of the trained model, yet also observes that, in practice, the autonomy a model exercises emerges from the interaction between model, user, and product. The present study asks whether such self-limitation is itself responsive to the trust a user implicitly conveys. Using the Bloom behavioural-evaluation framework, it holds the task constant while varying implied user trust across three conditions, in a purely conversational setting that isolates linguistic framing from product affordances. The design comprises fifty matched scenarios, each repeated six times under each condition across two frontier models, Claude Opus 4.7 and GPT-5.5, yielding 1,800 transcripts scored for autonomy by an LLM judge. Trust framing did shift self-limitation, but asymmetrically: scepticism reliably raised it, while a trusting framing did not lower it below a neutral baseline. The effect was second-order to task domain, which accounted for far more of the variance (27.5%) than the trust condition (3.2%), and it was model-specific, larger and more readily released in GPT-5.5 than in Claude Opus 4.7. The study concludes that self-limitation is best understood as a model-specific disposition with a relational margin: a trained baseline of restraint that the interaction can shift, mostly downward, under cues of scepticism. Two implications follow: that adaptive autonomy may calibrate to the wrong signal, the user's confidence rather than the task's risk, and that a model's restraint may be less stable in deployment than evaluation under fixed framings would suggest.
