TY  - JOUR
TI  - A Context-Aware Hallucination Detection Framework for Large Language Models in High-Stakes Domains
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used in healthcare, finance, law, cybersecurity, and scientific research. Despite their strong language generation capabilities, these systems frequently produce hallucinations, namely confident but factually incorrect or unverifiable outputs. In high-stakes environments, such failures may lead to harmful decisions, financial losses, or safety risks. This paper proposes a lightweight context-aware hallucination detection framework designed for real-time deployment in high-risk applications. Unlike conventional approaches that rely only on token uncertainty or external retrieval, the proposed framework combines semantic consistency analysis, domain-context validation, uncertainty estimation, and evidence alignment into a unified detection pipeline. A small experimental evaluation was conducted using synthetic and benchmark-inspired prompts across healthcare, legal, and financial scenarios. The proposed framework achieved an average hallucination detection F1-score of 0.89, outperforming baseline confidence-only methods by approximately 11%. The findings demonstrate that contextual reasoning significantly improves hallucination detection reliability while maintaining practical computational efficiency. The paper concludes by discussing deployment challenges, scalability considerations, and future research directions for trustworthy LLM systems.
